<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="so5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SO5: The Laplace Transform (Convolutions)</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the Laplace transform to compute the convolution of two functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
   
    <subsection xml:id="sec-convolution">
        <title>Convolutions</title>
        <definition xml:id="def-convolution">
            <title>Convolution</title>
            <idx><h>convolution</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given two functions <m>f(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m>, we define the <term>convolution</term> of <m>f(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m> to be the integral
                    <md>
                        (f*g)(t) = \int_0^t f(t-\tau)g(\tau)\, d\tau
                    </md>
                    for <m>t \gt 0</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <remark>
            <p>
                A few words about the convolution:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The convolution of two functions (of <m>t</m>) is again a function of <m>t</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The convolution of two functions intuitively tells us how one function <q>changes the shape</q> of another. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The Laplace transform of a convolution is the product of Laplace transforms, which is an unusual (but helpful!) property to have.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul> 
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The convolution of the functions <m>f(t) = t</m> and <m>g(t) = k</m> (for constant <m>k</m>) is given by 
                    <md>
                        <mrow>(t*k)(t) \amp = \int_0^t k(t-\tau)\, d\tau</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k\int_0^t t-\tau \, d\tau </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k \left[t\tau - \dfrac{1}{2}\tau^2\right]_0^t </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = k \left[(t^2 - \dfrac{1}{2}t^2) - 0\right] </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \dfrac{k}{2}t^2 </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the convolution of <m>f(t) = e^t</m> and <m>g(t) = t</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-convolution-laplace">
        <title>Convolutions &amp; The Laplace Transform</title>
        
        <assemblage>
            <title>Laplace Transform of a Convolution</title>
            <p>
                For piecewise continuous functions <m>f(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m>, 
                <md>
                    \mathcal{L}(f(t)*g(t)) = \mathcal{L}(f(t))\mathcal{L}(g(t)) = F(s)G(s)
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                This directly implies another characterization of the convolution, namely that 
                <md>
                    f(t)*g(t) = \inv{\mathcal{L}}(F(s)G(s))
                </md>
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We can compute <m>e^t*\cos(t)</m> by using the above statement.                  
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>\mathcal{L}(e^t) = \dfrac{1}{s-1}</m> and <m>\mathcal{L}(\cos (t)) = \dfrac{s}{s^2+1}</m>, so
                    <md>
                        F(s)G(s) = \dfrac{s}{(s-1)(s^2+1)}
                    </md>
                    After partial fraction decomposition, we get
                    <md>
                        F(s)G(s) = \dfrac{1/2}{s-1} - \dfrac{(1/2)s}{s^2+1} + \dfrac{1/2}{s^2+1}
                    </md>
                    so that
                    <md>
                        f*g = \inv{\mathcal{L}}\lrpar{\dfrac{1/2}{s-1} - \dfrac{(1/2)s}{s^2+1} + \dfrac{1/2}{s^2+1}}
                    </md>
                    which is given by
                    <md>
                        f*g = \dfrac{1}{2}(e^t - \cos (t) + \sin (t))
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>
</section>